COMMAND NAME: gpfdist

Serves external table files to Greenplum Database segments.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************


gpfdist -d <directory> [-p <http_port>] [-q <quote_character>] 
        [-x <escape_character>] [-h] [-v]

gpfdist -? | --version

*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************


gpfdist is a utility that serves external table files to all 
Greenplum Database segments in parallel. The benefit of using 
gpfdist over other file protocols is that you are guaranteed maximum 
parallelism while reading from external tables, thereby offering the 
best performance as well as easier administration of external tables.

In order for gpfdist to be used to serve external table files, the 
external table definition (as defined by CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE) must 
specify the directory and files (wildcards OK) that contains the external 
table data using the gpfdist:// protocol. 

When Greenplum Database users select from an external table, gpfdist 
parses and serves the data files evenly to all the segment instances in 
the Greenplum Database system.

Most likely, you will want to run gpfdist on your ETL machines rather 
than the hosts where Greenplum Database is installed. To install gpfidist 
on another host, simply copy the utility over to that host and add gpfdist 
to your $PATH.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************


-d <directory>

The directory from which gpfdist will serve files. If not specified, 
defaults to the current directory.


-p <http_port>

The HTTP port on which gpfdist will serve files. Defaults to 8080.


-q <quote_character>

Specifies the quote character for files in CSV (comma-separated values) 
format. If not specified, gpfdist will not parse for CSV and assume that 
each record in a file is separated by a newline character.


-x <escape_character>

Sets the escape character to use when parsing files. The default is 
quote_character.

-h (files have header row)

Specifies that each file being served has a header row. The first row 
of each file will be skipped. This option is only allowed for files 
in CSV format. 


-v (verbose mode)

Runs gpfdist in verbose mode.


--version 

Prints out the version of this utility.


-? 

Displays online help.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Serve files from a specified directory using port 8081 (and start 
gpfdist in the background):

gpfdist -d /var/load_files -p 8081 &


Serve files that are in CSV format and have a header row:

gpfdist -d /var/load_files -p 8081 -q '"' -h &


To stop gpfdist when its running in the background:

--First find its process id:

ps ax | grep gpfdist

--Then kill the process, for example:

kill 3456


